<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Fleming</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Alexander Fleming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Alexander Fleming was a Scottish physician and microbiologist, born at Lochfield farm near Darvel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire">Ayrshire</a>, who spent his career at St Mary's Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Serving in the Royal Army Medical Corps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, he showed that antiseptics often worsened deep wounds, and in 1922 he discovered the enzyme lysozyme. In 1928 he found that the mould Penicillium rubens produced a substance later named benzylpenicillin, the first antibiotic substance discovered. He was knighted in 1944 and shared the 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/howard-florey">Howard Florey</a> and Ernst Chain. He died of a heart attack at his home in London in 1955, and his ashes are buried in St Paul's Cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-fleming.jpg" alt="Alexander Fleming" width="330" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Official photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Synthetic_Production_of_Penicillin_TR1468.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fleming was born on 6 August 1881 at Lochfield farm near Darvel, the third of four children of the farmer Hugh Fleming and Grace Stirling Morton. His father was 59 when he married her and died when Alexander was seven. After Kilmarnock Academy he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, attended the Royal Polytechnic Institution and worked four years in a shipping office. An inheritance from an uncle and the example of his elder brother Tom, already a physician, led him to enrol at St Mary's Hospital Medical School in 1901. He qualified with distinction in 1906 and joined the research <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> under Sir Almroth Wright, a pioneer of vaccine therapy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Commissioned in 1914, Fleming served throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> in the Royal Army Medical Corps, and with Wright and Leonard Colebrook worked at the British military hospital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne-sur-Mer</a>. In a 1917 article in The Lancet he explained that antiseptics worked on the surface but that deep wounds sheltered anaerobic bacteria from them and the antiseptics removed protective agents; most army physicians kept using them. In late 1921 he found that nasal mucus inhibited bacterial growth and named the agent lysozyme, though his report to the Medical Research Club and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> aroused little interest. He became Professor of Bacteriology at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1928.</p><p>On 3 September 1928 Fleming returned from a holiday in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> and found that a culture of staphylococci was contaminated with a fungus, with the colonies around it destroyed. He remarked &quot;That's funny&quot; and identified the mould as Penicillium. The source of the spores was established in 1966 as the room of Charles J. La Touche, directly below Fleming's laboratory. In 1942 he used samples prepared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/howard-florey">Howard Florey</a>'s Oxford team to treat Harry Lambert, who had streptococcal meningitis; press coverage mentioned only Fleming, which he called the Fleming myth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fleming married the nurse Sarah Marion McElroy in 1915, and after her death in 1949 he married his colleague Amalia Koutsouri-Vourekas in 1953. He died of a heart attack at home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 11 March 1955. Knighted in 1944 and elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1943, he shared the 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/howard-florey">Howard Florey</a> and Ernst Chain, and in 1951 he became Rector of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. His laboratory at St Mary's is preserved as the Alexander Fleming Laboratory Museum. The popular story that Churchill's father paid for his education is false, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> was treated in 1943 with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulfonamide">sulfonamide</a> drug rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penicillin">penicillin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander Fleming” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Back from holiday, <b>Alexander Fleming</b> found a mould killing bacteria on a culture plate in his London laboratory.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-28">28 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
